<feed> tree:member <http://vocab.nerc.ac.uk/collection/L22/current/TOOL2310/1/> .
<http://vocab.nerc.ac.uk/collection/L22/current/TOOL2310/1/>
    a skos:Concept ;
    dc:isVersionOf <http://vocab.nerc.ac.uk/collection/L22/current/TOOL2310/>;
    dc:modified '2026-06-09T12:20:16'^^xsd:dateTime ;
    skos:altLabel 'Mirion Germanium Well series'^^xsd:string ;
    skos:prefLabel 'Mirion {Canberra} High-Purity Germanium (HPGE) Well Detector series'@en ;
    skos:definition 'A series of germanium detectors for ionising radiation, particularly x rays and gamma rays, with a well-shaped detector geometry. The blind well shape improves absolute efficiency for small samples as the sample is virtually surrounded by active detector material with the counting geometry approaching 4 pi. These instruments are semiconductor diodes with an intrinsic (i) region that is sensitive to ionising radiation as well as a p electrode and an n electrode. When the diode operates under reverse bias an electric field extends over the intrinsic or depleted region. When photons interact with the material within the depleted volume of the detector, charge carriers are produced and are swept by the electric field to the p and n electrodes. This charge is in proportion to the energy deposited in the detector by the incoming photon. The charge is converted to a voltage pulse by an integral charge sensitive preamplifier. The Intelligent Preamplifier (iPA) unit included in these detectors employs dynamic charge restoration (RC feedback) and provides excellent resolution and count rate performance. The preamplifier can be set up and tuned using digital controls. The thin, ion-implanted contact on the detector element inside the well allows spectroscopy from 20 keV up to 10 MeV. Models differ in detector size and well diameters. These detectors must be cooled to maintain the energy resolution of the detector, so instruments are available to suit a range of associated cryostat configurations.'@en ;
    skos:notation 'SDN:L22::TOOL2310'^^xsd:string .
